<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gripsholm Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1537</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gripsholm Castle stands on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malaren">Lake Mälaren</a> at Mariefred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-i-of-sweden">Gustav Vasa</a> built it from 1537 on the site of a medieval fortress, and it served as a royal residence and a state prison: Duke John and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-jagiellon">Catherine Jagiellon</a> were held there from 1563, and later the deposed Eric XIV. It houses the Swedish national portrait collection.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gripsholm-castle.jpg" alt="Gripsholm Castle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xauxa Håkan Svensson · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gripsholms_slott_view2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
